A1. What arguing with a wife is. ~ USELESS
A6. This kind of a car makes walking a necessity. ~ SMASHED
A11. A speak-easy. (Careful now!) ~ WHISPER
A12. Implore. ~ ENTREAT
A13. A loosely woven bunting like fabric. ~ ETAMINE
A14. A kind of summer pie. (Summer pies and summer not.) ~ RHUBARB
A16. An ancient exclamation. ~ EGAD
A19. Quench. ~ SLAKE
A20. Soil. ~ LOAM
A22. This makes all the stops. ~ LOCAL
A24. An English donkey. ~ ASS
A25. A close friend of the A. E. F. ~ LOUSE
A26. French admission. ~ ENTREE
A28. A cat that earns its board and room. ~ MOUSER
A29. A bird of a time. ~ LARK
A30. This always comes after a hard night. ~ DAWN
A31. Reason. ~ MOTIVE
A34. This means "to make negative." ~ NEGATE
A37. What nearly all hermits want to be. ~ ALONE
A38. It is (contr.). ~ TIS
A40. When married men start stepping out there's usually this fellow to pay. ~ DEVIL
A41. Mob. ~ GANG
A42. Something live wires have. ~ VOLTS
A44. This was started in the Garden of Eden. ~ RACE
A45. Something all clinging vines need for their support. ~ TENDRIL
A47. Fishing. ~ ANGLING
A48. A mouth, or an opening, or an open mouth. ~ ORIFICE
A49. A fellow who gets around a lot. ~ ROUNDER
A50. Present style in bathing suits. ~ EXTREME
A51. In dental offices these are not what they're cracked up to be. ~ ENAMELS

D1. Instrument of torture. (The second letter is K.) ~ UKELELE
D2. What dentists do when they have it out with a patient. ~ EXTRACT
D3. A water jug. ~ EWER
D4. Persian potentates. ~ SHAHS
D5. A Greco-Egyptian vase shaped like a bucket. ~ SITULA
D6. Generally speaking, this is what a woman generally does. ~ SPEAKS
D7. The busiest part of a taxi. (Be careful which way you spell it.) ~ METRE
D8. A camel jockey. ~ ARAB
D9. Extremely wicked, enot mous. ~ HEINOUS
D10. A rider of nightmares. ~ DREAMER
D15. Low down, sometimes the bottom. ~ BASE
D17. Venetian taxicab. ~ GONDOLA
D18. One dearly beloved. (Before marriage.) ~ DARLING
D20. A loafing lounge lizard. ~ LOUNGER
D21. Surgically clean. ~ ASEPTIC
D23. Something poker players seldom do when they should. ~ LEAVE
D25. Mooed. ~ LOWED
D27. Before. ~ ERE
D28. What a Swedish boy grows up to be. ~ MAN
D31. A great man, or a person with plenty of shekels. ~ MAGNATE
D32. This comes after this afternoon. (Two words.) ~ TONIGHT
D33. This kind of a woman is one who has no place to go. ~ WILD
D35. Cupidity. (Nothing to do with Cupid.) ~ AVARICE
D36. Lucky numbers. (Ask the man who rolls one.) ~ ELEVENS
D38. A sharp weapon. (Feminine.) ~ TONGUE
D39. Walked pompously, like a head waiter or bootlegger. ~ STRODE
D42. Poison. ~ VENOM
D43. A vamp of the H2O. ~ SIREN
D45. Good place for a blow-out. ~ TIRE
D46. In Italy this will buy nineteen cents' worth of bananas. ~ LIRA


